::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. What shared topic do the arguments address? {
~Whether roads should be closed.#Neither argument says roads should close.
=How the city should protect people from weather while traveling.#Correct. Both arguments address public comfort and safety during travel.
~Whether trees are always harmful.#Argument A supports trees.
~Whether buses should stop running.#Argument B supports bus shelters, not ending buses.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. What is Argument A's claim? {
~The city should build bus shelters first.#That is Argument B's claim.
~Residents never walk.#Argument A focuses on walking.
=The city should plant trees along the main road.#Correct. That is the position Argument A supports.
~Rain is the only concern.#Argument A focuses on hot months and shade.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. What is Argument B's reasoning? {
~Trees improve shade for walkers.#That is Argument A's reasoning.
~Bus shelters should be avoided.#Argument B supports bus shelters.
~Walking is never safe.#Argument B focuses on people waiting outside.
=Covered shelters would protect residents who wait for buses in heat and rain.#Correct. This explains why Argument B supports shelters.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. Which argument is more focused on immediate daily need? {
=Argument B, because it addresses residents waiting outside every day in heat and rain.#Correct. The daily waiting detail makes the need immediate.
~Argument A, because trees grow instantly.#Trees do not provide instant shade.
~Neither, because both arguments lack claims.#Both arguments have claims.
~Argument A, because it ignores weather.#Argument A addresses hot months.
}

::ENG3_U03_L04_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.08] Arguments: Argument A says the city should plant trees along the main road because shade will make walking safer during hot months. Argument B says the city should build covered bus shelters first because many residents wait outside in heat and rain every day. Which evaluation is strongest? {
~Argument A is wrong because trees are boring.#This is personal preference, not evaluation.
~Argument B is wrong because shelters have roofs.#That detail supports, not weakens, the reasoning.
=Both arguments are valid, but Argument B may be more immediately effective because it protects people already waiting for buses every day.#Correct. This evaluates reasoning and immediacy.
~Both arguments are identical because they mention weather.#They address related but different solutions.
}
